NEX patch for go-gost/go-shadowsocks2 v0.1.1, applied by gost-runtime/build.sh.

Makes gost's Shadowsocks client interoperate reliably with standard servers (sing-box,
shadowsocks-rust). Root causes are explained in comments next to each change.
  1. Classic AEAD salt filter (shadowaead/bloomring.go): the Bloom filter's double hash
     used FNV-1 and FNV-1a of the same input, which are strongly correlated; the real
     false-positive rate was ~0.6% instead of 1e-6 once the ring filled. Every false
     positive drops a packet / connection as "repeated salt". The UDP client adds one
     salt per packet, so UDP egress lost ~0.06% of replies after 10 minutes at 200 pps
     and would settle around 0.5%. Now seeded maphash.
  2. SS2022 UDP (shadowaead_2022/packet.go): the session subkey was derived from the
     session ID encoded little-endian; SIP022 uses the on-wire (big-endian) bytes.
     sing-box rejected every packet and gost could not have decrypted any reply.
  3. SS2022 TCP (shadowaead_2022/stream.go): the client decoded the response header as a
     request header and ignored its length. Per SIP022 that length is the size of the
     first payload chunk, which follows WITHOUT a length prefix; the first reply chunk
     was misread and failed authentication. Also checks the header type and the echoed
     request salt. A length of 0 (what gost's own server sends) keeps the old path.
  4. SS2022 UDP padding: only short DNS packets are padded (as sing-box does) instead of
     0-899 random bytes on every datagram, which fragmented 1200-1400 byte packets.
  5. utils.GeneratePadding returns 1..900 bytes; 0 made an SS2022 request without
     initial payload invalid (about one nodelay connection in 900 failed).

diff -ruN a/shadowaead/bloomring.go b/shadowaead/bloomring.go
--- a/shadowaead/bloomring.go
+++ b/shadowaead/bloomring.go
@@ -1,21 +1,27 @@
 package shadowaead
 
 import (
-	"hash/fnv"
+	"hash/maphash"
 	"sync"
 
 	"github.com/riobard/go-bloom"
 )
 
-// simply use Double FNV here as our Bloom Filter hash
+// NEX (nex-shadowsocks2.patch): upstream fed the Bloom filter's double hashing with
+// FNV-1 and FNV-1a of the same input. The two are strongly correlated, so the k probe
+// positions collapse onto few bits and the filter's real false-positive rate is orders
+// of magnitude above the configured 1e-6: measured with random 32-byte salts it was
+// about 0.07% after 300k entries and 0.6% once the ring is full (1M entries), against
+// ~0.0003% with independent hashes. Every false positive is reported as a "repeated
+// salt" and the packet / connection is dropped. A UDP client adds one salt per packet,
+// so a classic-AEAD UDP egress at a few hundred packets per second reaches that level
+// within minutes and keeps losing ~0.5% of its replies (and some TCP connections).
+// Two independently seeded maphash values (y forced odd so the stride never degenerates)
+// restore the intended rate; the seeds are per process, like the filter itself.
+var bloomSeedX, bloomSeedY = maphash.MakeSeed(), maphash.MakeSeed()
+
 func doubleFNV(b []byte) (uint64, uint64) {
-	hx := fnv.New64()
-	hx.Write(b)
-	x := hx.Sum64()
-	hy := fnv.New64a()
-	hy.Write(b)
-	y := hy.Sum64()
-	return x, y
+	return maphash.Bytes(bloomSeedX, b), maphash.Bytes(bloomSeedY, b) | 1
 }
 
 type BloomRing struct {
